/* What is a Delegate in C#?

A delegate in C# is a type-safe reference to a method. It stores the reference of a method and allows that method to be called through the delegate.

Purpose of Delegate
It is used to pass methods as arguments.
It provides flexibility in calling methods.
It is widely used in events and callback functions.
A delegate can point to one method or multiple methods.
General Syntax
delegate ReturnType DelegateName(parameters);

Example:*/


public delegate void MyDelegate();

static void Display()
{
    Console.WriteLine("Hello");
}

static void Main()
{
    MyDelegate d = Display;
    d();
}
